Why? Had the cat gone wild? If that was the case, it wasn't _your_ cat
anymore ;)

> >> and started to sac somemore, in hopes of pleasing my
> >> god.

This was highly unlikely to work - an angry god requires _big_
sacrifices to be mollified, and on Dlvl one, it'll be hard to
impossible to have sufficiently big creatures generated. If you try
this again, look for an altar deeper in the dungeons.

> >> I did so unsuccessfully, and for some reason ended up praying
> >> again,

Praying by accident really shouldn't happen. If this option isn't
activated yet, turn on "prayconfirm". If it is on, be a bit more
careful and don't say "y"es to every question ...

> Killing a pet is always a very bad idea, and nothing to be laughed about.

Killing a pet gone wild shouldn't give you a problem. And even killing
a tame creature doesn't cause too much trouble (unless you kill it by
displacing it into an unsafe spot).

> >> Jack the Woman-at-arms      St:17 Dx:14 Co:18 In:8 Wi:8 Ch:10  Lawful
> >> Dlvl:1  $:5500 HP:0(95) Pw:33(33) AC:-2 Xp:11/10538 T:17552 Strained
> >                                                              ^^^^^^^^
> 
> > First mistake - until you get quite expert, make sure you are *never*
> > burdened or worse. That makes you slower, and a slow adventurer can
> > become a dead adventurer very quickly.
> 
> Indeed. Burdened is bad, stressed is worse, but strained is inexcusable.

Looking through the inventory, the strained status was definitely
caused by the ape corpse that was lugged around. Since the character
wasn't anywhere near the altar, i'm absolutely unsure why it was
carried - trying to crawl there while strained would have been
extremely hazardous (as it turned out) and possibly futile - the
corpse would probably have started to rot by the time the altar was
reached.

Carrying a corpse to an altar that's more than 5 paces away is only
feasible if the weight doesn't burden you beyond "stressed".
Everything beyond it is by far too risky, and as soon as _anything_
hostile shows up, even a mere rothe, your first move should be getting
rid of the excess weight. Don't allow _anything_ to attack you while
you're worse than burdened. Not dropping the dead ape the very moment
the fire ant became visible was a really, really bad move.

> However, the original poster was going to the up stairs to leave the game,
> so he probably wanted to take as much scoring loot with him as possible.

Certainly not the dead ape. Anyway, since you can't go upstairs when
stressed or worse ...

> >>    c - an uncursed +3 small shield (being worn)
> >>    y - an uncursed +0 pair of elven boots (being worn)
> >>    A - an uncursed +0 bronze plate mail (being worn)
> >>    T - an uncursed +0 helmet (being worn)
> 
> > Plate mail is very heavy and generally best avoided if there is any
> > choice at all.
> 
> Completely agreed. He might have fallen back from strained to just
> burdened only by dropping this single item.

Possibly. Dropping the stupid ape, however, might have made the
character un-burdened. Plate mail isn't quite as heavy as many people
seem to think, but i'd certainly recommend taking lighter armour, even
simple studded leather, instead of bronze plate. Makes weight
management a whole lot easier. Losing a point or two of AC is worth
less than going from burdened to unburdened, or just freeing two
hundred or more units of carrying capacity.

Also: no cloak? You really should've had a chance to get one by the
time, and it would have made the fire ant a lot less efficient by
blocking many of its "you are on fire!" attacks.

> I *never* wear plate mail. I prefer to go without body armour instead, if
> I find nothing else. The speed difference means you'll get hit almost
> twice as often when you are wearing one, thus making you lose *more* HP on
> average, instead of less.

There's no inherent speed difference linked to plate mail. You can
stay unburdened with plate mail on, it just takes a bit more inventory
maintenance (and a bag of holding is almost mandatory).

> >>    i - a wand of create monster (0:10)
> 
> He should have used this one at the altar, healing back to full HP between
> monsters. This would probably have gotten him Mjollnir.

Not with an angry god. And on dlvl 1, it might have been insufficient
to appease Tyr.

